3.text:
In the mean field the $v_{k}$ and $u_{k}$ processes can be replaced by their
low pass filtered stationary moments

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k},
\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl} ,
\end{equation*}
and, with $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\rangle =0$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle =\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$,
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
     \Big[\mu^{2}+\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence for a common weight $w$ and a common rate $\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle =0\iff
 \phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big[\mu^{2}+\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}\big]
   +\rho\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0  .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{kl}=c$ for $k\ne l$ and $c_{kk}=1$ put

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}=1+\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\equiv S ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]
     +\rho\mu S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)=-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S-1  .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}
      =-\frac{\mu^{2}}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}-
        \frac{\rho}{\phi}\frac{\mu}{\sigma_{x}^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
the zero mean plasticity condition can be written in the familiar
form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}\; ,
\end{equation*}
which is the quoted $c^{*}=\big(\kappa S-1\big)/(S-1)$.
For the usual normalisation $\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle
=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$, the inessential additive
constant can be absorbed in the definition of $S$ and one obtains the
customary dimensionless threshold

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\;\propto\;\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional sign $\rho\to-\rho$,
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$, the normalised Hebbian offset
$v_{k}^{\rm thr}=-\rho$.  With the sign convention $ \dot w_{k}
\propto u_{k}\big(v_{k}-\theta\big)$ this is most often quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\approx
1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=\big[\,\kappa_{*}S-1\big]/(S-1)$ is the mean–field
correlation necessary for a net positive $\langle\dot w\rangle$.  In
particular with the additive constant absorbed the conventionally quoted
form is $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa\simeq-\rho/\phi\mu$ with the
usual $ \rho\to-\theta$ convention, which is the normalised
LTP/LTD threshold $-\rho/\phi\mu$ often written as $\kappa=
(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  This is the $-\rho/\phi\mu$ threshold that is
usually quoted in the literature. ∎

5.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be replaced by their stationary
means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\ ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l} ,
\end{equation*}
and, for a stationary rate process,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi  \sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}
 \Big\langle x_{l}x_{k}\Big\rangle
 \simeq \phi\mu^{2}w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
 \Big[\,1+c_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
up to the usual $ \tau_{u}\tau_{v}$ overlap factor which can be absorbed
in $\phi$.  For the completely homogeneous state $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu $,
$w_{k}\equiv w$ and $c_{kl}\equiv c$ for $k\ne l$ we may put $s_{kk}=1$ and
$c_{kk}=1$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big[1+c_{kl}\big]
 =S_{k}+ \sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
 =S_{k}+1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\,,
\qquad S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S .
\end{equation*}
With $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle=0$ in the mean,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\mu^{2}w\Big[\,S+1+c(S-1)\Big]+\rho\mu wS .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with the sign convention $\rho<0$ for LTD,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\big[S+1+c(S-1)\big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=
\frac{-\,S\!\left(1+\rho/\phi\mu\right)-1}{\,S-1\,}
=
\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the overall sign which is conventionally absorbed in
$\rho$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless LTD–LTP ratio.  In the sign convention used in
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ this is often written as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1
      =\frac{|\rho|-\phi\mu}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $ \kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\rho<0$, is the conventional
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ LTD threshold.  With the usual LTD convention
$\rho\to|\rho|>0$, $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho)$, this is
$\kappa=1-\rho/\phi\mu$, the form quoted in the literature as
$\kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  This is the ratio which controls the
$1/(S-1)$ term and, for $0<\kappa<1$, $0<c^{*}<1$ is the familiar
$ c^{*}=( \kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ clustering threshold.  The minus sign is
the usual convention $\rho<0$ for depression; with the opposite sign
convention $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is the quantity quoted.

1.text:
In the mean field the two low pass filters can be replaced by their
means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\;,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
     \Big[\,1+{\cal I}\;c_{kl}\Big] ,
\qquad 
{\cal I}\equiv \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)h_{u}(s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v}(t)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-t/\tau_{v}},\;h_{u}(t)=\tau_{u}^{-1}
e^{-t/\tau_{u}}$ and ${\cal I}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ for the usual
white–noise spike train.  Putting $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho \langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi \mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\,1+{\cal I}c_{kl}\Big]+\rho
\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
For a uniform line $s_{kk}=1$, $c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}=S,\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=c\,(S-1)+1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+{\cal I}\big(c(S-1)+1\big)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with the conventional overlap ${\cal I}=1$ the
mean–field balance can be written

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big(1+\rho/\phi\mu\big)+1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless LTP/LTD balance

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
          =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the usual one–change of sign gives the often quoted
form $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the
filter overlap explicit the effective balance parameter which enters the
stability condition is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}= -\frac{1+ \rho/\phi\mu}{{\cal I}}
               =-\Bigl(1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}) ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional ${\cal I}=1$ normalization
$\kappa_{\rm eff}\propto 1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  In particular, up to the
inconsequential sign convention, the balance which controls the
clustering instability is the familiar LTD/LTP ratio

\[
\kappa\;\propto\;1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\]

i.e. $\kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, the minus sign being absorbed in the
definition of the LTD offset $\rho<0$.  With the sign convention used in
the derivation above this is $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$,
the often quoted $c^{*}=[\kappa S-1]/(S-1)$ form.  Removing the
inconsequential overall sign this is the usual $ \kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
balance parameter, and with the filter overlap retained
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$, whose
${\cal I}=1$ limit is the expression quoted in the literature. ∎

