2.text:
In the mean field put $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$ and

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad
\langle u_{k}\rangle = w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
   \propto \big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle  .
\end{equation*}
For white spike trains
$\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\,1+{\cal I}c_{kl}\Big]$,
${\cal I}=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}h_{v}$ and with the conventional
${\cal I}=1$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[1+c_{kl}\Big]+\rho\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
On the line $s_{kk}=1$, $c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}=S,\qquad
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
Hence the zero–mean fixed point is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{\,S\!\left[1+\rho/\phi\mu\right]+1\,}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{a}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{a}= -\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
       =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\;{\rm for}\;\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
which is the signed quantity which multiplies $S$ in the statement
$c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ in the literal convention
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$.

With the usual LTD sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\theta)$
$\rho\to-\theta<0$ the overall minus sign is absorbed in the definition
of the offset and the positive LTP/LTD balance which is normally quoted
is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{+}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\approx-\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential $\rho\to-\rho$ sign flip
$c^{*}=(\kappa_{+}S-1)/(S-1)$ with the familiar normalised Hebbian
threshold $v_{k}^{\rm thr}=-\rho$.  With the filter overlap kept
explicit ${\cal I}=\int h_{u}h_{v}=(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ in the
unit–area normalisation the effective balance which enters the zero mean
condition is $\kappa_{\rm eff}=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)/{\cal I}
\propto-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$, which reduces to the
${\cal I}=1$ form $\kappa\propto1+\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above.  In
particular on the $\dot w\propto u(v-\theta)$ convention
$\kappa_{+}\simeq-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the usual $-\rho/\phi\mu$ LTD/LTP
ratio.  This is the $\kappa_{a}=-\kappa_{+}$ quoted in the signed
form $c^{*}=(\kappa_{a}S-1)/(S-1)$ above. ∎

